.TH smi-pki-client-extensions "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-client-extensions \- Write client certificate OpenSSL extensions to a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-client-extensions
.I OUT_EXT_FILE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Writes the client extensions for mutual TLS to \fIOUT_EXT_FILE\fR: basicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE, keyUsage=critical,digitalSignature, extendedKeyUsage=clientAuth, subjectKeyIdentifier=hash and authorityKeyIdentifier=keyid,issuer.
There is no subjectAltName; the client is identified by its Common Name.
keyUsage is digitalSignature alone, because a TLS client only signs the handshake, which is also correct for EC keys.
It is a thin command line wrapper over the matching function in the file based PKI library
\fBpki.sh\fR, so each step of building a CA and signing certificates can be run and inspected
on its own. All material is passed as file names; the command fails if the underlying OpenSSL
operation fails.
